The uncentered intercept, 18.8 mm², is higher than anything we measured in sham animals at day 0 (14.6 mm²), because it extrapolates a line fitted on the way down from the day-3 bump. The centered intercept, 16.8 mm², sits where the data are.

## What I believed before looking

@sec-priors-regularization taught us that leaving a prior at its default is a decision, so this time I make it on purpose. In my own data and in the ischemia models I have worked with before, hemispheric tissue shrinks on the order of 1 mm² per day through the first two weeks. That number comes from my experience and my earlier data, not from a paper, and I would rather say so than dress it up. So the slope prior is centered at $-1$ mm² per day, with a small spread: a standard deviation of 0.5 mm² per day. Written on the scale of the outcome, that prior says three things. A loss of 2 mm² per day sustained over 11 days would remove more tissue than any section in this file contains (the largest is 20.2 mm²), and the prior gives it little weight. A *gain* is nearly ruled out, since only about 2% of the prior probability sits above zero. And everything between no change and a loss of 2 mm² per day is allowed to compete (@tbl-single_continuous_priors).






::: {#tbl-single_continuous_priors .cell tbl-cap='Priors for the tissue-loss model, with their reading on the scale of the outcome'}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Smallest and largest section in the file, to ground the priors
area_range <- range(cell_data$Tissue_Area)

# The three priors and what each means on the scale of tissue area
gt(data.frame(
  Parameter = c("Intercept (area at day 3)", "Slope (mm² per day)", "sigma (spread around the line)"),
  Prior = c("Normal(15, 4)", "Normal(-1, 0.5)", "Exponential(0.5)"),
  Reading = c(
    sprintf("95%% of belief between 7 and 23 mm²; the sections in this file run from %.1f to %.1f mm²", area_range[1], area_range[2]),
    "About 95% of belief between a loss of 2 mm² per day and no change",
    sprintf("Mean of 2 mm²; only %.0f%% of belief above 6 mm², an implausible typical distance from the line when areas run from %.1f to %.1f mm²", 100 * exp(-0.5 * 6), area_range[1], area_range[2])
  )
))
```

