:::






The conclusion does not depend on my prior. Even a prior that starts from *no effect* ends within a few hundredths of the others (@tbl-single_continuous_sensitivity), so the data, not my experience, carry this inference. That is worth knowing, because with other datasets it will not always be true.

::: {.callout-note title="How to report it"}
**Results.** Within the window from day 3 to day 14 after ischemia, hemispheric tissue area fell by 0.67 mm² per day (95% credible interval 0.53 to 0.82). Expected area was 16.8 mm² at day 3 and 9.4 mm² at day 14, a loss of about 44% (95% credible interval 36% to 51%).

**Methods.** We fitted a Gaussian linear model in `brms` with the day centered at 3, so the intercept is the area at day 3. The slope prior was Normal(-1, 0.5) mm² per day, based on earlier work in this model; the intercept and the residual standard deviation had weakly informative priors. Replacing the slope prior with a skeptical Normal(0, 0.5) changed the estimated slope by 0.02 mm² per day across the three analyses. Sampler diagnostics were clean (largest Rhat 1.002, no divergent transitions). A posterior predictive check showed a single residual spread is too wide for days 7 and 14 and too narrow for day 3, so intervals for individual animals should not be read from this model.
:::

::: callout-tip
### Frequentist equivalent

`brms` and `lm` agree to the second decimal, and now we can say why. With 31 animals the likelihood is far sharper than my prior: the prior carries only about 1.9% of the weight in the slope, so the posterior is almost exactly what `lm` maximizes on its own [@gelman2020]. The `lm` row is also, in effect, the answer a flat prior would give, so the table doubles as a check against the fully noncommittal analysis. The divergence you will see later in the book shows up with hierarchical structure, tiny samples, or priors that do real regularizing work; none of those applies yet to this single-level model.
:::

## Is the line honest about the spread?

Now the promise I made at the table of days: the spread at day 3. The model has one `sigma` for the whole window, so it assumes every animal scatters around the line by the same amount. @fig-single_continuous_residuals checks that, as @sec-linear-regression-assumptions taught us to: not with a test, with a picture.






::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Residual of every animal (posterior mean prediction subtracted), by day
resid_df <- data.frame(
  DPI = factor(cell_data_sub$DPI),
  residual = residuals(brms_model)[, "Estimate"]
)

# Spread per day, and the day-3 spread without its single lowest animal
res_sd <- tapply(resid_df$residual, resid_df$DPI, sd)
res3 <- resid_df$residual[resid_df$DPI == "3"]
res3_sd_trimmed <- sd(res3[res3 > min(res3)])

# Every residual as a point; the dashed line at zero marks a perfect prediction
ggplot(resid_df, aes(x = DPI, y = residual)) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0, linetype = "dashed", color = "grey40") +
  geom_jitter(width = 0.15, height = 0, color = "#0072B2", alpha = 0.8, size = 2) +
  labs(x = "Days post-ischemia (DPI)", y = "Residual (mm²)") +
  Plot_theme
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Residuals of the tissue-loss model by day (mm²)](linear-regression-single-continuous_files/figure-html/fig-single_continuous_residuals-1.png){#fig-single_continuous_residuals width=528}
:::
:::






The residuals at day 3 spread with a standard deviation of about 3.1 mm², against 1.1 at day 7 and 1.0 at day 14. Subsetting fixed what it was meant to fix, the linearity, but not this. Part of the day-3 spread is one animal about 6.3 mm² below the line (the lone low point in @fig-single_continuous_fit), and with eight animals a single unusual mouse and a genuinely wider day are hard to tell apart. Without that animal the day-3 standard deviation falls to 1.8 mm², still well above the 1.0 to 1.1 of the other days, so some of the excess is a genuinely wider day and some is one unusual mouse, and with eight animals I cannot say how much is which. The single `sigma` (1.8 mm²) averages over a noisy day 3 and two calm days, so it is too large for days 7 and 14 and too small for day 3 as we observed it. The slope itself is not in question, since its credible interval sits far from zero, but I would not trust the model to predict where a *new* day-3 animal will land. The direct fix is to let `sigma` depend on the day, a distributional model, which is what @sec-multivariate-distributional-regression does; @sec-appendix-assumption-tests shows what that change does on the full-range data.

@sec-mcmc-model-checking introduced the posterior predictive check as the way to ask whether a fitted model's simulated data resemble the real thing. A density overlay would not show the day-3 problem, so I check the quantity that matters: for each day, the standard deviation of the observed animals against the standard deviations of datasets the model replicated (@fig-single_continuous_ppcheck). The figure comes from `ppc_stat_grouped()` in `bayesplot`, which lets you pick the statistic and the grouping; choosing the statistic you are worried about is what makes a posterior predictive check informative.






::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
library(bayesplot)

# 500 replicated datasets from the fitted model
yrep <- posterior_predict(brms_model, ndraws = 500)

# Per day: SD of the real animals (line) against SDs of the replicated datasets (bars)
ppc_stat_grouped(
  y = cell_data_sub$Tissue_Area,
  yrep = yrep,
  group = factor(cell_data_sub$DPI),
  stat = "sd"
)
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Posterior predictive check of the standard deviation by day (observed value as a line, replicated datasets as histograms)](linear-regression-single-continuous_files/figure-html/fig-single_continuous_ppcheck-1.png){#fig-single_continuous_ppcheck width=672}
:::
:::








The two ends of the figure pull in opposite directions, which is what a single `sigma` forces. At day 3 the observed standard deviation (3.1 mm²) sits in the far right tail of what the model replicates: only about 1% of replicated datasets scatter that much at day 3. At days 7 and 14 the observed values (1.1 and 1.0 mm²) sit at the left edge, with about 95% of replicates scattering at least that much at each of them, because the model expects about 1.8 mm² everywhere. The model's one `sigma` is a compromise that is wrong in both directions. A density overlay would have looked fine and missed all of this, which is why the check should be built around the thing you suspect.

Two coefficients, an honestly scoped window, a prior I can defend and a named limitation: that is a complete linear regression, start to finish. @sec-linear-regression-multiple picks up the natural next question: what happens once we bring a second predictor into this same model? And further out, @sec-longitudinal-timeseries returns to the *entire* 0 to 30 day range we set aside, this time with a model built to represent a rise followed by a fall, instead of subsetting the problem away.
